<div class="content-section">

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>BNEsim</strong> if you want flexible, no-expiration data packs at a low per-GB price and don't need calls or texts included. Pick <strong>Orange</strong> if you're traveling in Europe and want unlimited calls, SMS, and hotspot bundled in standard, backed by one of Europe's biggest telecom groups, and you're fine paying more for that. Neither has a working discount code right now: both build their markdowns into the sticker price instead.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Data-Only Flexibility vs a Carrier's All-In-One Bundle</h2>
  <p>
    BNEsim sells per-country and per-region data packs with a real choice built in: a cheaper version that expires in 30 days, or a pricier version of the same pack that never expires. That matters if you visit a country irregularly rather than on a fixed schedule, since the data just sits ready until you actually land. It also runs on a credit-based account that accepts a wider range of payment methods than most providers on this site, crypto included, and lets you schedule a plan to start activating on a future date instead of the moment you buy it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Orange Travel is the international eSIM arm of Orange, one of Europe's largest telecom groups, and its Europe plans include unlimited calls and SMS plus hotspot tethering by default, not as a paid add-on the way most data-only eSIMs on this site sell it. That coverage runs on 700+ roaming partner networks. Its own site names 39 specific European countries and territories on the Europe plan we checked, not just a vague "200+ destinations" claim. None of that is cheap: Orange sits in our "Expensive" price bracket, while BNEsim sits in our "Cheap" bracket.
  </p>

