    <p class="content-note">These aren't directly comparable: BNEsim prices per gigabyte with no calls or SMS included, while Orange sells fixed calls+SMS+hotspot bundles that get automatic strike-through markdowns as the plan gets bigger and longer. A light data-only European trip is almost always cheaper on BNEsim; a longer stay where you actually want calls and texts working can make Orange's bundle worth the higher price.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Discount Codes</h2>
  <div class="content-box content-box--tan">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>BNEsim:</strong> No working code found. Discounts of up to 70% are already built into the sticker price as an automatic strike-through markdown. <a href="/bnesim/">Full details &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><strong>Orange:</strong> No working code found either. The code we previously listed, AW0, doesn't appear on the real site and is marked invalid. <a href="/orange/">Full details &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Both providers use the same pricing pattern: bigger or longer plans get a bigger automatic discount, with no coupon field to hunt for. Compare the sale price shown on each site directly rather than looking for a code that isn't there.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is BNEsim or Orange the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with BNEsim if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You visit a country irregularly and want data that doesn't expire while you wait</li>
        <li>You'd rather pay with crypto or run a top-up account balance than check out fresh every trip</li>
        <li>You only need data, not calls or texts, and want the lowest per-GB price</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Orange if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You're traveling in Europe and want calls and SMS included standard, not bought separately</li>
        <li>You're booking a longer stay (up to 90 days) where the bigger automatic markdowns actually pay off</li>
        <li>You want the backing of an established carrier and a full refund if the eSIM goes unused</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>BNEsim lets you schedule a future activation date.</strong> Orange's plans activate as soon as you set them up through the QR code or app, so there's no equivalent way to buy now and lock in a start date weeks out the way BNEsim's scheduling does.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Orange's calls-and-SMS bundle isn't available a la carte from BNEsim at any price.</strong> BNEsim is data-only across its entire catalog, so if voice and texting matter for a trip, no BNEsim plan at any tier gets you there; Orange or a similar carrier-backed provider is the only route.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide. Want a data-only provider with an actual working discount code instead? See <a href="/airhub/">Airhub</a> or <a href="/redteago/">RedteaGO</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>BNEsim vs Orange FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, BNEsim or Orange</h3>
      <p>BNEsim, for data-only use. Its per-GB pricing (as low as $0.95/GB in Europe) undercuts what Orange effectively charges once you factor out the calls/SMS/hotspot bundle Orange includes automatically.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either have a working discount code</h3>
      <p>No, neither does right now. Both build their savings into the sticker price as an automatic markdown instead of a checkout code: BNEsim's scales up to 70% off, Orange's scales up to 40% off on its biggest Europe plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one includes calls and texts</h3>
      <p>Orange, standard on its Europe plans, along with hotspot tethering. BNEsim is data-only across its whole catalog with no calls or SMS option at any tier.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I buy either one ahead of a trip without the clock starting early</h3>
      <p>Yes for BNEsim, which lets you schedule a plan to activate on a future date you choose. Orange's eSIM activates once you install it via QR code or the app, so there's less room to lock in a start date weeks in advance.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

